WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.162
This video goes under Android: partitions, Treble, how Android boots, and how to add your own vendor HAL with the right SELinux policy.

00:00:08.562 --> 00:00:19.789
Treble: Android's split between Google's system part and the vendor's hardware part, so each can update separately. Like: A building with a fixed wall between the landlord's floors and the tenant's floors.

00:00:20.089 --> 00:00:33.208
HAL: Hardware Abstraction Layer: a fixed interface Android calls, which the device maker implements for its hardware. Like: A standard wall socket: Android plugs into the same socket, and each maker wires the house behind it.

00:00:33.508 --> 00:00:46.976
SELinux: Security-Enhanced Linux: rules that say exactly which program may touch which file or device, even as root. Like: Keycards in an office: even the boss's card only opens the doors it is programmed for.

00:00:48.076 --> 00:00:53.892
An Android system update ships, and suddenly the camera service on the bar can't open the camera.

00:00:54.142 --> 00:00:57.613
The system part and the vendor part disagreed about their interface.

00:00:57.863 --> 00:01:00.929
Let's see how Android keeps those two halves stable.

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:04.893
Treble is like a building with a fixed electrical panel.

00:01:05.293 --> 00:01:07.626
The apartment is the Android framework.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:15.782
Framework layer: On Android, the Java system services apps talk to, like the camera and USB services. Like: The dining room of a restaurant: diners (apps) only ever see this room.

00:01:16.032 --> 00:01:19.422
The walls and wiring are the vendor's HALs and drivers.

00:01:19.822 --> 00:01:24.443
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:01:24.693 --> 00:01:28.582
Between them are standard plugs: versioned HAL interfaces.

00:01:28.982 --> 00:01:34.195
So Google can replace the apartment with a new Android version, without rewiring the walls.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:36.091
Words.

00:01:36.491 --> 00:01:39.278
A O S P is the Android open source project.

00:01:39.678 --> 00:01:44.299
AOSP: Android Open Source Project: the public Android source code a device maker builds from. Like: The public recipe book every Android kitchen starts from.

00:01:44.599 --> 00:01:51.019
Treble, with the vendor interface manifest, splits system and vendor with stable, versioned interfaces.

00:01:51.419 --> 00:01:56.237
An A I D L HAL is a hardware interface defined in the A I D L language.

00:01:56.637 --> 00:02:08.607
AIDL: Android Interface Definition Language: the file that describes the calls between two Android processes. Like: A menu both the waiter and the kitchen agree on, so orders are never misunderstood.

00:02:08.907 --> 00:02:14.526
Init dot r c is the language Android's init uses: services, triggers and properties.

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:22.159
init / .rc files: The first user program Android starts; its .rc files say which services start and when. Like: The stage manager with a script that says who walks on stage and when.

00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:30.594
SELinux is mandatory access control. Every process and file has a label, and every access must be explicitly allowed.

00:02:30.994 --> 00:02:36.021
And G K I is the generic kernel image. Vendor code must live in loadable modules.

00:02:36.421 --> 00:02:42.726
GKI: Generic Kernel Image: one Google-built Linux kernel shared by many devices, with vendor drivers loaded as modules. Like: A standard car chassis from the factory, with each brand bolting on its own parts.

00:02:43.026 --> 00:02:49.748
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:02:50.798 --> 00:02:53.062
Android's boot, step by step.

00:02:53.462 --> 00:02:58.001
The bootloader verifies v b meta, which covers the boot and vendor images.

00:02:58.401 --> 00:03:05.019
Bootloader: The small program that runs first at power-on, sets up memory and loads the kernel. Like: The stagehand who sets up the stage before the actors arrive.

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:09.669
The generic kernel starts, with vendor modules from the vendor boot partition.

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:15.352
First stage init mounts the system and vendor partitions, and loads the remaining modules.

00:03:15.752 --> 00:03:18.933
The SELinux policy is loaded, in enforcing mode.

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:25.173
Second stage init parses the r c files from system and vendor, and runs triggers in order.

00:03:25.573 --> 00:03:29.880
Service manager starts, then the HAL services, including your own.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.647
And finally zygote, system server, and the apps.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.704
Here are the lab eleven files.

00:03:37.104 --> 00:03:43.083
The interface is marked VINTF stable, with two methods: set U S B functions, and is streaming.

00:03:43.483 --> 00:03:53.201
VINTF: The vendor interface manifest: the list that says which hardware services a device provides. Like: A restaurant's posted list of dishes it promises to serve.

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:58.795
The r c fragment tells init to start the service in the hal class, as the system user.

00:03:59.195 --> 00:04:05.186
The type enforcement file gives the service its own SELinux domain, and lets init launch it into that domain.

00:04:05.586 --> 00:04:09.603
And one narrow rule lets it write configfs files, nothing more.

00:04:10.003 --> 00:04:23.981
configfs: A special folder tree where you build a USB gadget by creating folders and writing small files. Like: Building with LEGO by making folders: each folder is a brick (a function), and linking them snaps the toy together.

00:04:25.081 --> 00:04:26.916
A safe SELinux workflow.

00:04:27.316 --> 00:04:30.834
Search the logs for a v c denials, and read each one.

00:04:31.234 --> 00:04:35.599
Add the narrowest rule that fixes it. Never ship a permissive domain.

00:04:35.999 --> 00:04:39.319
Audit two allow gives a starting point, but not the answer.

00:04:39.719 --> 00:04:45.838
Never allow rules are checked by the compatibility test suites, so broad rules fail certification.

00:04:46.238 --> 00:04:52.194
And under G K I, you can't edit the core kernel. Your drivers become modules against a stable symbol list.

00:04:53.394 --> 00:04:56.633
A few daily commands. Check USB properties.

00:04:57.033 --> 00:04:58.879
Dump the USB service state.

00:04:59.279 --> 00:05:01.195
Check that your HAL registered.

00:05:01.595 --> 00:05:03.580
Watch all logs and the kernel log.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.372
And build and flash only the vendor image.

00:05:07.572 --> 00:05:13.562
Question for you: Why does Android require the vendor part to talk to the system through fixed interfaces?

00:05:18.862 --> 00:05:26.293
Answer: So Google's system part and the device maker's hardware part can be updated separately without breaking each other.

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:28.089
Recap.

00:05:28.489 --> 00:05:31.984
Treble connects framework and vendor through versioned HALs.

00:05:32.384 --> 00:05:36.772
Boot goes from bootloader, to the generic kernel, to init, to HALs, to zygote.

00:05:37.172 --> 00:05:43.082
A HAL needs an interface, a service, an r c file, a manifest entry, and policy.

00:05:43.482 --> 00:05:45.966
Write only narrow SELinux rules.

00:05:46.366 --> 00:05:48.712
And vendor kernel code lives in modules.
